#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093495 :tutorial/content "An **indicator function** is a function that maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1$, depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset.\n\nThe indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X$. Then, the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\in A[/math], \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x \\notin A[/math]\n$$\n\nFor example, let\n\n$$\nA = \\{a,c \\}\n$$\n\nbe a subset of the universal set\n\n$$\nX=\\{a,b,c,d,e \\}\n$$\n\nThen, the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_A(x)$ of $A$ is the following:\n\n![](images/7b/7b1ec0a80d5ec7dc9974d2ebaf6984e94faa00d73ec624dc48d56addbe701ee1.png)\n\nNotice that the elements $a$ and $c$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1$, and all other elements are mapped to $0$." :tutorial/id #uuid "20986866-6719-520f-b707-cf6b26e3823b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8006 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093496 :tutorial/content "Recall that the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\in A[/math], \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x \\notin A[/math]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the indicator function of the complement $\\overline{A}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\in \\overline{A}[/math], \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x \\notin \\overline{A}[/math]\n$$\n\nThis means that the indicator function of $A$ and its complement are related as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1_{A^(―)}(x) &= 1 - 1_{A}(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the indicator functions of $A \\cap B$ and union $A \\cup B$ can be expressed through the indicator functions of $A$ and $B$.\n\n- For the indicator function of the *intersection*, we have:\n$1_{A∩B}(x)|= min{1_{A}(x), 1_{B}(x)}; = 1_{A}(x) \\cdot 1_{B}(x)$\n- For the indicator function of the *union*, we have\n$1_{A∪B}(x)|= max{1_{A}(x), 1_{B}(x)}; = 1_{A}(x) + 1_{B}(x) - 1_{A}(x) \\cdot 1_{B}(x)$.\n\nWe can prove these results by splitting them into cases." :tutorial/id #uuid "a111c445-39e3-5ddd-8272-48ef3795a3b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8007 :tutorial/title "Properties of Indicator Functions"}]}
